course: dictionary
lesson: 7780
title: Tall towers and power lines
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: A pylon is a tall metal tower that holds up thick electricity cables high above the ground. Look at the clues in the sentence: «Pylons are tall steel towers that hold high-voltage wires high above the ground.» Tall, steel, wires and high above the ground all point to one thing, so a pylon is a tower for power lines.
explain: Context also tells you what to do. «Never climb a pylon or into an electricity substation, even for a ball.» The words never and even for a ball show that a pylon is dangerous to climb. Always ask what word would fit, then try your meaning in the sentence to check it still makes sense.
explain: Some words have more than one meaning, and the context tells you which one is right. In some places, such as the US, a pylon is a traffic cone. If the sentence is about roads, cones and drivers, use that meaning. If it is about wires and power, use the tower meaning.
fact: A pylon is a tall metal tower that holds up thick electricity cables.
fact: «Pylons» are tall steel towers that carry «high-voltage» wires, which means wires carrying a very strong electrical push.
fact: Electricity travels long distances at very high voltage, because less of it is wasted that way.
fact: In the US, a pylon can also mean a traffic cone.
fact: Never climb a pylon, and never touch a fallen wire: only engineers should work near power lines.
exercise: «Pylons are tall steel towers that hold high-voltage wires high above the ground.» What is a pylon? | A small wooden hut for tools | A type of bicycle | A pond full of fish | *A tall metal tower that holds up electricity cables
exercise: «Never climb a pylon, even for a ball.» Why must you never climb one? | *Because it is dangerous, and only engineers should work near the wires | Because pylons are soft and might break | Because pylons are only for tall people | Because the ball is inside the tower
exercise: Find and copy one word from «It travels along thick cables on tall pylons at very high voltage, a very strong electrical push.» that means a strong electrical push. | pylons | *voltage | thick | cables
test: In the US, a pylon can mean a traffic cone. What does pylon mean in a sentence about roads and drivers? | A bridge over water | A metal tower for wires | *A traffic cone | A power station
test: «Electricity travels long distances at very high voltage because less is wasted that way.» Why is electricity sent at very high voltage? | So that more people can climb the towers | So the wires look bigger | *Because less electricity is wasted | Because high voltage makes the wires cooler
exam: «A substation lowers the voltage before the electricity reaches our streets and houses.» What does a substation do? | *It lowers the voltage before electricity reaches homes | It is a tall tower for kites | It makes the voltage much higher | It climbs the pylons to fix the wires
